 from the board  um and eat it a good distance away from  my opponent that brings me to another  point yes you are allowed to get up from  the board and yes technically you can  get up on your turn you can get up on  your opponent's turn but to go to the  bathroom that's another thing that you  should do before the first round you  should find where the nearest bathroom  is uh so you don't have to waste any  time looking but the best way is you  make a move you press the clock you get  up to go to the bathroom you can also  get up to watch other games in progress  that kind of stuff  um there is no prohibition there's no  limit to the amount of times you can get  up and again you want to get up  discreetly making as little noise as  possible  um but beverages  are allowed and you know light food and  food items are allowed i once played  next to somebody who brought sushi and  it was of the particularly smelly  variety i think it was eel  let's just say that individual was not  very well well liked by people sitting  around his board so make sure you're  exercising common sense and good  judgment deciding what food to bring  um okay castling yeah so uh castling is  done normally you want to make sure  you're doing  is that you are touching your king first  you're putting it here  and you'll bring the rook round do not  touch the rook first if you touch the  rook first yes your opponent can force  you to move the rook this is very  important for older players  i i think particularly a lot of older  russian players they tend to move the  rook first i don't really know why  but uh i think even anatoly karpov  former world champion was  had an incident like this where he moved  the rook first make sure you're moving  the king and moving the rook away  the other thing is that you want to do  everything with one hand if possible all  right  technically what's prohibited is moving  with one hand and then pressing the  clock with the other not that you would  necessarily do that  but you want to make sure you're doing  everything with one hand that simplifies  the process all right  if you want to castle with two hands  sometimes in a blitz game you can do  this but what you do not want to do is  have one hand moving the other hand  pressing the clock that looks weird i'm  not sure that there's an actual rule  against it but it is highly frowned upon  make sure that you're moving with one  hand there's no move that you need two  hands for all right  um  so that's just an etiquette thing  all right what else do i have declined  draw we talked about resigning we talked  about castling beverages getting up  yeah so i have  flag fall that's easy to explain and  then three-fold repetition okay so let's  say your opponent is lost on time all  right one very important thing  there are tournaments that have a sudden  death time control in tournaments that  do not a sudden death time control just  means that a certain amount of time is  added after a certain uh move number is  played for example it's usually move 30  or move 40. some clocks they if  the sudden uh death time control move  has been made they will go down to zero  before adding the additional 30 moves  make sure that you're well acquainted  with  what number move you're at and that's  why it's so important to keep notation  carefully  and the type of clock that you have all  right you don't want to call flag if  your opponent hasn't actually lost on  time but if your opponent has lost on  time it's going to show zeroes it is  your responsibility and yours alone  to indicate that your opponent is lost  on time you want to do is you want to  look over and say time  and your opponent will most likely  acknowledge it if they do not  acknowledge it then you stop the clock  and you call the td but again that's not  gonna happen  um it's pretty rare to lose on time but  it does happen so to indicate that your  opponent is lost on time you whisper  time and  handshake the game is over  t